[17592186519450 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519450 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02b0f97c-a1c4-5de3-9e6c-ca0b67ea4390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186369469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519631 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48cb7736-a399-57ef-8551-b03d268a4144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186369455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7855bb63-c81b-53d8-a3eb-d50bbb715b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186441618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b29823e-1d02-5228-87de-cfdd6eddac82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186431351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519667 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dcca2676-1c39-51b2-9647-11d87db779a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186519559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538179 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538151 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/57/573013be1dc56a1ae4d0765b81b8e94006b8402a6da9706b118d43e286fb8291.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0093dc0f-6d13-5136-8a41-4ec719538cf8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538151 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi}{2}  \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi}{2} .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538154 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538156 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1e97d150acbe82bf31d0a0e371122ae567d62279b66a5db2a3164180248a5e71.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28489039-fdfa-503c-aed0-769a751abddb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538156 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {5}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (25\\pi -16\\pi ) \\\\  & =108\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538209 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538161 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/24/245fb5eea54d303236a038f3d1b8253bc6c2e70426bcf0e5f48a40704b47d669.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c303577-d49b-5f6d-ae6a-7c78499f5f3d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538161 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=9y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-9)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (16\\cdot 12-{4}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (192-16-36) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-560.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538193 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538171 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d1b2d83da42a1a95525ce3359e947800af884cd29f1ead6d9dbe4cee1c8dfb40.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c5f644e-f3a9-5505-9151-ceb09a68b40b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538171 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=-6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (16\\cdot 10-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (160-16-4) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-1960.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538170 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538181 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/48/483ce10aae9978fbc4c1b5a71173ab10b52bca0b15c84f01d7adb7247e82e436.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "874f8d64-a3fc-5a12-89b8-9d65bb0715ce" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538181 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-(-2))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 48\\pi  \\\\  & =240\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538184 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538203 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538184 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538184 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4a/4a1b8a23b4fb0d081f9d88748735e02b3a173843e5c4a1a40e70e6ba4e7335ad.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538184 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9687dcec-a46d-5800-a061-342d9450fae3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538184 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-6y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-6))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (14\\cdot 8-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (112-16-4) \\\\  & =14\\cdot 92 \\\\  & =1288.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538192 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538195 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538192 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538192 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d2039c49121a2da30efa96b7d456a043aa3860bc19399e67385c3f3268f6c0f8.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538192 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa14acb3-297f-5aab-a401-2c7b97eae563" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538192 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}25\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {9}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (81\\pi-9\\pi-4\\pi) \\\\  & =25\\cdot 68\\pi \\\\  & =1700\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538174 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538200 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/83/83357e8db109cd09ddcc51043caff588225edfd1b8f7b98dbb640a230ea7843d.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf10ccdd-2f45-595c-a4ea-08a81d57feab" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538200 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (49\\pi -36\\pi ) \\\\  & =78\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538207 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538186 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538207 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538207 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c9/c92f6dbb417fb4ff7bff5442d50d57777c74a9072c13bbc614f333f76e04d7f2.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538207 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e22aa2a8-a144-5ad0-98b1-7417fbf28999" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538207 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (49\\pi-4\\pi-9\\pi) \\\\  & =19\\cdot 36\\pi \\\\  & =684\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538214 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133886.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538214 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "e4c2e450-4ea3-5474-8dcb-399f4aa636fe" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543812 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543813 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543812 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543812 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{23}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543812 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a13fd10-2736-5163-8e91-3a139268a2d1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543812 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-(- \\frac{17}{2})+(- \\frac{19}{3})+\\frac{23}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-51)+(-38)+46}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543811 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543825 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9be43ff2-1cbd-5518-ad47-108fe13ce12b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543825 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{3}+(- \\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-38+(-21)+57}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543808 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{25}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{13}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca868da8-dd46-5274-aa8f-98c77655ddad" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543832 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =(- \\frac{25}{3})-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{13}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-50)-32-39}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{121}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543834 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543823 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543834 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543834 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{1}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8e9f007-9cb8-5437-b13d-dccd4da1a757" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543834 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}-(- \\frac{19}{3})+(- \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{51-(-38)+(-2)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543824 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543836 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{14}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{20}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0468836-7eb8-505a-98df-3234db3c120b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543836 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-(- \\frac{7}{2})+(- \\frac{14}{3})-\\frac{20}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-21)+(-28)-40}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{47}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543841 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133899.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543841 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "a98a12de-5c53-5bc9-88b2-2d0c424935b9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558971 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558932 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19907c37-a4a5-570e-a985-43945896ae6b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558932 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):2\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{2}^{5}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(4\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((2)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(16\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 609 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558934 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558943 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}$, calculate $\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b6c6718-cecd-5d44-9775-b44c08c7c182" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558943 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 175 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558951 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186558949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 175921